  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d9a614c5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Experiment 1: Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Adding the same constant to every Bradley–Terry parameter changed the numerical parameter values but did not change any pairwise probability. Therefore, comparison data identify only parameter differences, not their common absolute level.\n",
    "\n",
    "The normalization\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\theta_i=0\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "removes this additive ambiguity by selecting one centred representative from the family\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\left\\{\n",
    "\\boldsymbol{\\theta}+c\\mathbf{1}\n",
    ":\n",
    "c\\in\\mathbb{R}\n",
    "\\right\\}.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This normalization makes the parameter representation identifiable, but it does not by itself guarantee that a finite MLE exists. Existence additionally depends on the observed winner-to-loser structure, which is studied next."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6e21857e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Experiment 2: Finite MLE and Ford's Condition\n",
    "\n",
    "Two different graphs arise from pairwise-comparison data.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Observed comparison graph\n",
    "\n",
    "An undirected edge connects \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) whenever they were compared, regardless of the outcome:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "i-j.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This graph records which comparisons occurred.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Directed win graph\n",
    "\n",
    "A directed edge points from the winner to the loser:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "i\\rightarrow j\n",
    "\\quad\\text{if item }i\\text{ defeated item }j.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This graph records the directions of the observed outcomes.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the standard Bradley–Terry model, a finite MLE exists when the directed win graph is strongly connected. This means that every item can reach every other item through a sequence of directed wins.\n",
    "\n",
    "Equivalently, for every nonempty proper subset \\(S\\) of items, there must be an observed win from \\(S\\) to its complement and an observed win in the reverse direction.\n",
    "\n",
    "This requirement is known as Ford's condition.\n",
    "\n",
    "The following experiment constructs a dataset satisfying Ford's condition and examines the resulting NLL and parameter trajectories."
   ]
  },
