  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60626fc0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Experiment 2: Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "Ford's condition holds because the directed win graph is strongly connected. Every item can reach every other item through observed winner-to-loser paths.\n",
    "\n",
    "During optimization:\n",
    "\n",
    "- the negative log-likelihood decreases and approaches a finite value;\n",
    "- every parameter trajectory settles at a finite level;\n",
    "- the parameters remain centred, with their sum equal to zero.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, the numerical behaviour is consistent with the theory: when Ford's condition holds, the normalized Bradley–Terry MLE exists as a finite and unique solution.\n",
    "\n",
    "These plots provide numerical evidence for this dataset; Ford's condition provides the mathematical guarantee."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "40588390",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### experiment 3: When a Finite MLE Does Not Exist\n",
    "\n",
    "In this experiment, every pair of items will be compared, so the ordinary comparison graph will be complete and connected. However, the outcomes will follow a perfectly separated ordering:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "A \\succ B \\succ C \\succ D \\succ E.\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Whenever two items are compared, the higher-ranked item always wins. The directed win graph therefore contains paths from stronger items towards weaker items, but no path in the reverse direction. It is not strongly connected, so Ford's condition fails.\n",
    "\n",
    "Gradient descent can continually improve the likelihood by increasing the distance between the stronger and weaker parameters. For example, consider\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boldsymbol{\\theta}(t)\n",
    "=\n",
    "t(2,1,0,-1,-2).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "As \\(t\\) increases, the probability assigned to every observed winning outcome approaches one. The negative log-likelihood approaches its infimum, but the parameter magnitudes continue growing.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, the likelihood has no finite maximizing parameter vector even though the undirected comparison graph is connected."
   ]
  },
